Setting `retry: {limit: 0}` **does** change how the request body is handled. It prevents Ky from cloning the request body.

-   When retries are enabled (`limit > 0`), Ky clones the request body using `ReadableStream.tee()` to ensure the body can be re-sent on a subsequent attempt. This process buffers the entire stream in memory (`readme.md:405-406`, `readme.md:1578-1579`).
-   By setting `retry: {limit: 0}`, the request is not cloned. This avoids buffering and is recommended for sending large streaming bodies when retries are not needed (`readme.md:406`, `readme.md:1579`).
-   The implementation for this logic is in `source/core/Ky.ts`:
    ```typescript
    // source/core/Ky.ts:946-951
    const retryRequest = this.#options.retry.limit > 0 ? this.request.clone() : undefined;
    const request = this.#wrapRequestWithUploadProgress(this.request, this.#options.body ?? undefined);

    // Cloning is done here to prepare in advance for retries.
    // Skip cloning when retries are disabled - cloning a streaming body calls ReadableStream#tee()
    // which buffers the entire stream in memory, causing excessive memory usage for large uploads.
    ```

